On a flex container, justify-content aligns items on the main axis and align-items on the cross axis. To center both ways: display:flex; justify-content:center; align-items:center; (or the grid one-liner: display:grid; place-items:center).

```css
.center {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* main axis */
  align-items: center;       /* cross axis */
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

```css
.banner {
  background: linear-gradient(270deg, #8b5cf6, #ec4899, #f59e0b, #8b5cf6);
  background-size: 600% 600%;
  animation: shift 12s ease infinite;
}
@keyframes shift {
  0%   { background-position: 0% 50%; }
  50%  { background-position: 100% 50%; }
  100% { background-position: 0% 50%; }
}
```

Why it works: a CSS gradient is treated as an image, so it can be sized and positioned. The gradient is painted at 600% of the element width — far larger than what is visible — and the animation slides background-position across it. Because the first and last color stops are the same (#8b5cf6 ... #8b5cf6) and the keyframes return to the starting position, the loop is seamless with no visible jump. Animating background-position instead of the gradient itself means no repaint of new images per frame; the browser just moves an existing painted layer, which stays cheap.

