```css
*,
*::before,
*::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

.panel {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 2rem;
  border: 4px solid #2563eb;
  /* total rendered width stays 100% of parent */
}

/* content-box equivalent: rendered width = 100% + 4rem + 8px,
   which overflows its container */
```

**Why this works:** The default `box-sizing: content-box` makes the `width` property describe only the content area; padding and border are then *added on top*, so an element declared `width: 300px; padding: 20px` actually renders 340px wide. `border-box` redefines `width` to describe the final border edge — padding and border eat into the declared width instead of growing it. This matches how humans reason about sizes ("a 300px card is 300px wide") and makes systems like `width: 50%` plus padding compose predictably: two 50% elements plus a gap fit in one row instead of wrapping. The universal selector with `::before`/`::after` covers pseudo-elements too, and the rule is inherited-safe because `box-sizing` is not inherited — hence the explicit `*` reset rather than setting it on `<body>`.

