```js
document.querySelectorAll('.item').forEach(item => {
  const head = item.querySelector('.head');
  const body = item.querySelector('.body');
  head.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const open = head.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
    head.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!open));
    if (open) {
      body.style.height = body.scrollHeight + 'px';
      requestAnimationFrame(() => { body.style.height = '0px'; });
    } else {
      body.style.height = body.scrollHeight + 'px';
      body.addEventListener('transitionend', function done(e) {
        if (e.propertyName === 'height') {
          body.style.height = 'auto';
          body.removeEventListener('transitionend', done);
        }
      });
    }
  });
});
```

Why it works: CSS cannot transition to height:auto, so the pattern is: measure scrollHeight, set it as an explicit pixel height (which is transitionable), then on open flip it to auto after the transition ends so the panel resizes with content later. Closing does the reverse — pin to the measured height first, then drop to 0 inside requestAnimationFrame so the browser registers the start value before the transition begins. aria-expanded keeps assistive tech informed, and overflow:hidden clips the content during the animation.

